id: pm-4020c5
title: "Dependency-kind lexicon: fifteen accepted spellings for roughly eight relations, canonicalized inconsistently at write time, so the stored graph vocabulary keeps fragmenting"
description: "The declared dependency-kind domain accepts fifteen spellings, enumerated by the mutation error on an unknown kind (2026.7.25):\n\n  blocks, parent, child, parent_child, child_of, related, related_to, discovered_from, blocked_by, incident_from, epic, implements, supersedes, task, verifies\n\nThose cover roughly eight distinct relations. The hierarchy family alone has five spellings (parent, parent_child, child_of, epic, task) plus the inverse child; the associative family has two (related, related_to). Two of them, epic and task, are item type words used as relationship kinds.\n\nCanonicalization is applied inconsistently. pm-fl0c shipped write-time canonicalization for one alias only: --dep kind=depends_on is stored as blocked_by. Every other alias is stored verbatim. Verified on disk in an isolated sandbox:\n\n  pm-3l9d,child_of,...\n  pm-sar6,epic,...\n  pm-3l9d,related_to,...\n\nThe graph registry then canonicalizes on read: with exactly those three rows on disk, pm graph audit reports edges_by_kind {parent: 2, related: 1}. No edge is lost to graph queries, and this item does not propose changing that read behaviour.\n\nThe cost lands on the two consumers that read the store directly. First, the documented composition story: pm data is plain text specifically so agents and operators can drive it with grep and jq, and a pipeline selecting kind == \"related\" silently misses every related_to row. Second, the native profile hides the fragmentation it is the natural place to surface: edges_by_kind has no related_to key, so an operator cannot see from any command that alias rows exist at all. On the repository tracker there are sixteen related_to rows against 2,189 related rows.\n\nThe vocabulary is still growing, so a data-side cleanup alone cannot converge. pm-bwnclq and pm-b1zsk9 were both created on 2026-07-25 carrying related_to rows, and pm-pegcmx carries four from 2026-07-21. pm-p9a4 owns classifying the existing aliases and pm-hnc9w7 owns retiring them, but neither closes the writer that keeps minting new ones. This is the relationship-kind analogue of the flag lexicon that pm-fs8q9x governs for flags: one concept, one canonical spelling, every historical spelling still accepted as a hidden alias."
type: Issue
